{
  "requirement": "Habeas MUST locate its per-user configuration at\n`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/habeas/config.toml` on Unix when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is an absolute,\nnonempty path, otherwise at `$HOME/.config/habeas/config.toml`. On Windows it MUST\nuse `%APPDATA%/habeas/config.toml`. If the required base directory cannot be\ndetermined, an operation requiring user configuration MUST fail with an\nexplanatory diagnostic.\n\nThe same user configuration MUST be usable across projects. Its location MUST\nNOT be selected relative to the current project or changed by project settings.\nUser-local preferences for a project MAY remain in the user configuration;\nworkspace settings MUST use `habeas.toml` under\n{cite}`requirements.configuration.workspace_location`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The implementation matches the requirement and is marked `{impl}` throughout. The tests cover the main cases. There are small gaps, listed at the end, but none is large enough to fail the requirement.\n\n**Implementation (`src/config.rs`)**\n- `user_config_path_from` (about lines 563–575) handles each platform:\n  - On Unix it uses `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` when that is absolute and nonempty. An empty path is never absolute, so it falls through. Otherwise it uses `$HOME/.config`.\n  - On Windows it uses `APPDATA`.\n  - In every case it appends `habeas/config.toml`.\n- `absolute_directory` rejects empty and relative values. `required_directory` returns a `NotFound` `io::Error` with the message \"cannot determine Habeas directory: {name} must be an absolute, nonempty path\". That is the explanatory diagnostic the requirement asks for.\n- The error propagates with `?` into operations that need user configuration. One example is the authoring resolution path near line 809, which calls `user_config_path()?`.\n- `user_config_path` reads the real environment, and `ConfigPlatform::current` selects the platform. Both are marked `{impl}`.\n- The path depends only on platform and environment variables. It is not selected relative to the current project and project settings cannot change it. A search for other uses of `user_config_path`, `APPDATA` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` found no code that does either.\n- Workspace settings are handled separately by `load_workspace_config`, which the `workspace_location` requirement covers.\n\n**Tests (`tests/configuration.rs`, `locations_use_absolute_xdg_values_and_platform_fallbacks`)**\n- An absolute `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` is used.\n- An empty `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` and a relative `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` each fall back to `$HOME/.config/habeas/config.toml`.\n- Windows uses `APPDATA`.\n- Unix with no variables set returns an error.\n- Other integration tests (`authoring_cli`, `authoring_batch`) set `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` and `APPDATA` and run the real binary, so the location is also exercised end to end.\n\n**Minor gaps, not enough to fail**\n- The test only asserts `is_err()` for the missing-base case. It does not check the diagnostic text.\n- A missing `APPDATA` on Windows is tested only through `state_root_from`, not `user_config_path_from`.\n- `HOME` and `APPDATA` are also required to be absolute. The requirement does not say this, but it is a reasonable reading of \"cannot be determined\".",
